# NorthNarrow — process comm allowlist (default, v1)
#
# Tappa 10.5 (D1). PROGRAM NAMES the process rules R006, R007, R011..R017
# and the io_uring and BPF rules (NN-L-IOU-*, NN-L-BPF-*) trust.
#
# An entry is matched on what the kernel executed (external audit
# NETR-003 class): the executable's basename, or the script's an
# interpreter runs (`.py` dropped), which must lie in a root-owned
# system tree (/usr, /opt, /bin, /sbin, /lib, /snap, /nix/store). A
# 15-character entry also covers the longer names the kernel truncates
# a process name to (`unattended-upgr` for `unattended-upgrade`). A
# process that only CALLS itself like an entry (prctl(PR_SET_NAME), a
# copied binary) is not trusted.
#
# What a match does (external audit PROC-008):
#   - ACTOR (what the entries below are). R011, R012, R013 and R017: a
#     root exec of a program in a system tree whose lineage holds an
#     entry — the entry ran its program before the process below it was
#     forked — is RECORDED (Log) instead of killed: DKMS running modprobe
#     under apt or dpkg. A program outside a system tree, or an exec by
#     another uid, is judged as if no entry were there.
#   - TARGET. The program a rule judges is itself an entry: no verdict,
#     anywhere on the host. The io_uring and BPF rules judge the calling
#     process this way (snapd's device-cgroup programs, for example).
#
# Format: one name per line; `#` introduces a comment; blank lines
# ignored; NO `+`/`-` prefixes (those are .local-only). Operators
# tune via the overlay at /etc/northnarrow/process-comm-allowlist.local
# with `+name` (add) / `-name` (re-enable detection on a default).
# Every disabled default emits a boot WARN.
#
# IMPORTANT — what NOT to put here: the detection targets themselves
# (insmod, modprobe, setcap, nsenter, runc, bash…). As a TARGET entry
# such a name silences its rule for that program on the whole host. The
# default below is limited to package-manager and configuration-management
# ACTORS.
# Ansible's remote modules run from ~/.ansible/tmp, outside any system
# tree, and never match: a per-workload identity allowlist (`+R013
# unit=…`, docs/design/TAPPA10_9_WORKLOAD_CONTEXT_DESIGN.md §4.3) is the
# planned way to scope those.

# Package managers
apt
apt-get
dpkg
dnf
yum
rpm
snapd
unattended-upgr

# Configuration management / provisioning
cloud-init
ansible
puppet
salt-minion
chef-client
